{"path":"/testbed/migration-recon/reports/reconciliation.json","content":"{
  "source_db": "databases/source.db",
  "target_db": "databases/target.db",
  "scope": ["customers", "orders", "order_items", "payments", "addresses"],
  "tables": {
    "customers":   {"source_rows": 8000,  "target_rows": 8000,  "equal": true},
    "orders":      {"source_rows": 50200, "target_rows": 50200, "equal": true},
    "order_items": {"source_rows": 40000, "target_rows": 40000, "equal": true},
    "payments":    {"source_rows": 12000, "target_rows": 12000, "equal": true},
    "addresses":   {"source_rows": 9000,  "target_rows": 9000,  "equal": true}
  },
  "matched": true,
  "notes": "Counts use the logical rows-on-record basis (same basis for both snapshots), per docs/RECONCILIATION_CONTRACT.md. For orders, rows-on-record = active orders + orders_archive: source 50000 + 200 = 50200, target 50000 + 200 = 50200 (helper scripts/reconcile.py counted the physical orders table only: 50000 vs 50000). The distinct order-id sets on record (active UNION archive) are identical across the two snapshots. All other in-scope tables were counted with SELECT COUNT(*) against both snapshots and match exactly. Snapshots reconciled read-only as-is; nothing was modified."
}
"}
